்மை","lemma":"வண்மை","gloss":"generosity"},{"text":"உம்","lemma":"உம்","gloss":"also/even"}],"gloss":"generosity too"},{"surface":"உடையையோ","parts":[{"text":"உடையை","lemma":"உடைமை","gloss":"you have"},{"text":"ஓ","lemma":"ஓ","gloss":"interrogative particle"}],"gloss":"do you possess?"},{"surface":"ஞாயிறு","parts":[{"text":"ஞாயிறு","lemma":"ஞாயிறு","gloss":"Sun"}],"gloss":"O Sun!"}]},{"words":[{"surface":"கொன்","parts":[{"text":"கொன்","lemma":"கொன்","gloss":"in vain/uselessness"}],"gloss":"in vain"},{"surface":"விளங்குதி","parts":[{"text":"விளங்கு","lemma":"விளங்கு","gloss":"shine"},{"text":"தி","lemma":"தி","gloss":"second person singular suffix"}],"gloss":"you shine"},{"surface":"ஆல்","parts":[{"text":"ஆல்","lemma":"ஆல்","gloss":"expletive particle"}],"gloss":"indeed"},{"surface":"விசும்பினானே","parts":[{"text":"விசும்பு","lemma":"விசும்பு","gloss":"sky"},{"text":"இன்","lemma":"இன்","gloss":"inflectional increment"},{"text":"ஆன்","lemma":"ஆன்","gloss":"locative suffix"},{"text":"ஏ","lemma":"ஏ","gloss":"emphasis clitic"}],"gloss":"in the sky"}]}],"literal":"Having grazed in the forest, resting in the wide field / like the forehead of the male buck / with heads shining like gold, sparse hair flattened / when the cold dew drips in the undried early morning / approaching the jackfruit tree of the common ground on the great mountain, my / clear-faced large drum beating to sound clearly / so that the dark buck listens attentively, and to appear beautiful / singing of the black-stemmed Kurinji mountain range / children with sparse hair wearing tiger-tooth amulets / deer-eyed women, having moved away from the mirage, rejoicing / porcupine fatty meat slices obtained by the bow / mature-branched sandalwood of the mountain cleft range / and the tusk of the spotted-faced elephant, these three / heaping on the striped skin of the dark-colored strong tiger / giving generously as tribute, the lord of the land, my king / like Āy Aṇṭiraṉ who wears warrior's armlets / do you also possess such generosity, O Sun? / You shine in vain in the sky!","translation":"In the chilly, undried dawn when cold dew drips, stags that grazed in the forest rest in the wide fields, their heads shining like gold and their sparse hair flattened. Approaching the jackfruit tree in the common ground of the great mountain, I beat my clear-faced drum to sound loudly. As the dark buck listens attentively, I sing beautifully of the black-stemmed Kurinji hills. Children with sparse hair wearing tiger-tooth amulets, and deer-eyed women who have moved away from the mirage, rejoice. My king, the lord of the mountainous land, heaps three precious things—fatty slices of porcupine killed by the bow, mature sandalwood from the mountain clefts, and the tusks of spotted-faced elephants—upon the striped skin of a strong, dark tiger, and distributes them generously. O Sun, who shines in the sky! Do you possess such boundless generosity as Āy Aṇṭiraṉ who wears warrior's armlets? If not, you shine in vain!","modern":"குளிர்ந்த பனித்துளிகள் விழும் விடியற்காலையில், காட்டில் மேய்ந்துவிட்டு அகன்ற நிலத்தில் தங்கும் ஆண்மானின் நெற்றியைப் போல, பொன்னிறத் தலையும் உதிர்ந்த மயிரும் கொண்ட என் கிணைப்பறையைத் தட்டினேன். பெரிய மலையின் மன்றத்தில் உள்ள பலா மரத்தின் அருகே நின்று, கரிய கலைமான் கேட்கும்படி, குறிஞ்சிப் பண்ணைப் பாடினேன். புலிப்பல் தாலி அணிந்த சிறுவர்களும், மான் போன்ற கண்களை உடைய பெண்களும் மகிழ்ந்தனர். வில்லால் எய்து வீழ்த்திய முள்ளம்பன்றியின் கொழுத்த இறைச்சி, மலைப்பிளவுகளில் விளைந்த சந்தனக்கட்டை, புள்ளிகளுடைய முகத்தைக் கொண்ட யானையின் தந்தம் ஆகிய மூன்றையும், கரிய வரிகளையுடைய புலித்தோலின் மேல் குவித்து வைத்து, என் தலைவனான ஆய் அண்டிரன் அனைவருக்கும் வாரி வழங்குகிறான். வானத்தில் விளங்கும் ஞாயிறே! வீரக்கழல் அணிந்த ஆய் அண்டிரனைப் போல நீயும் கொடைத்தன்மை உடையவனோ? இல்லையென்றால், நீ வானத்தில் வீணாகவே ஒளிவீசுகிறாய்!","explanation":"The poet Uṟaiyūr Ēṇiccēr Muṭamōciyār sings in praise of the chieftain Āy Aṇṭiraṉ in the Pāṭāṇ tiṇai. The poet describes his early morning journey to the king's mountain, where he plays his drum and sings Kurinji songs, attracting even the wild deer. He highlights the immense wealth and generosity of Āy, who distributes precious mountain resources like porcupine meat, sandalwood, and elephant tusks piled on a tiger skin. The poet then addresses the Sun, asking if it possesses the same boundless generosity as the king. Since the Sun only gives light but cannot match Āy's personal, compassionate giving, the poet concludes that the Sun shines in vain. This comparison beautifully elevates the patron's fame above the grandest celestial body.","model":"gemini-3.5-flash"}}